{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":true,"decision":"pass","reasons":[],"slug":"vmware-monitor","displayName":"vmware-monitor","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/zw008/skills/vmware-monitor","publisherHandle":"zw008","publisherDisplayName":"wei zhou","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/zw008","version":"2.4.2","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1792368941745,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/vmware-monitor/card?ownerHandle=zw008&version=2.4.2","sha256":"947a10893d4eef70e3f8c39b2ad3345d1b2d83fe66baa49335b6007d61eebfe5","size":2048,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"2e9ffc19750fe82794ad6f86f949c2917d749dde4d398830eecc997a30e723a7","bundleFingerprints":["2f421ca0bfcda01d116bbf05b6698d64e052c34d529c936c0490714ebae94604"],"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":25070,"sha256":"0ff359cc0c8b0622ed60f7ce5188e0def3533f41416903440ceb6f8d0407f336","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"evals/evals.json","size":1255,"sha256":"3b2746e29a48a3ff97d6c33c40eea6c79b26d8b6e2e1fbacfb7bd52518f4a8b4","contentType":"application/json"},{"path":"references/agent-guardrails.md","size":6781,"sha256":"9d524072391e27c821f7053a08d2342e1a8487a19d63cd51a4ae91cba9b33e6c","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/capabilities.md","size":22284,"sha256":"460fcdbae81b60489ab47321750a50a590fca2dd5a50be04d39ab45064340fbe","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/cli-reference.md","size":12459,"sha256":"23e4c9301c18270603e5fa05892c787c6251d599294264522e06cd598c194487","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/health-summary-template.md","size":9143,"sha256":"5209bb6cda1ba7af7e3d0f5c46854e92774fed961c50c26758aa4ee5d4affca5","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/investigation-protocol.md","size":6758,"sha256":"159bdcc5dfdf9b6414c548d7bf7c326dbb849e38883699a4a80cb76267c55893","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"references/setup-guide.md","size":18033,"sha256":"61ca7ba6f75ae0e9c83fe7769fc21c27c81bf144be663a6844c24d767ea341b2","contentType":"text/markdown"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"clean","passed":true,"rawStatus":"clean","verdict":"benign","confidence":"high","summary":"This is a clearly disclosed VMware monitoring skill with sensitive but purpose-aligned read-only infrastructure access and optional user-started notifications.","model":null,"checkedAt":1792369659481,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.2","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[],"properties":{"securityScore":100,"primaryLanguage":"Markdown","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1792369604.5644853,"endTime":1792369628.307413}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"vmware-monitor","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v571m1939smsh9t9dv32hhjed98gkq4f-A4as8M/clawscan-skillspector-1704757818/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-10-19T00:26:43.689725+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","max_issue_severity":"HIGH"},"components":[{"path":"SKILL.md","type":"markdown","lines":292,"executable":false,"size_bytes":25070,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"evals/evals.json","type":"json","lines":38,"executable":false,"size_bytes":1255,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"references/agent-guardrails.md","type":"markdown","lines":116,"executable":false,"size_bytes":6781,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"references/capabilities.md","type":"markdown","lines":317,"executable":false,"size_bytes":22284,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"references/cli-reference.md","type":"markdown","lines":194,"executable":false,"size_bytes":12459,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"references/health-summary-template.md","type":"markdown","lines":171,"executable":false,"size_bytes":9143,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"references/investigation-protocol.md","type":"markdown","lines":140,"executable":false,"size_bytes":6758,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null},{"path":"references/setup-guide.md","type":"markdown","lines":295,"executable":false,"size_bytes":18033,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null}],"structured_summaries":[],"issues":[{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-59aebb64c3ed4ac4affc2062cff346b8","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":17,"end_line":null},"finding":".env ","explanation":"The skill documents storing vCenter credentials in a local .env file and optional webhook transmission of sanitized operational data. Even though it says credentials are not sent and files are permission-restricted, local plaintext-equivalent secret storage and outbound notifications expand the exposure surface if the host is compromised or logs/webhooks are misconfigured.","remediation":"Prefer OS keychain/secret-manager storage over .env files, avoid reversible obfuscation such as base64, minimize credential lifetime, and document exact redaction guarantees for webhook payloads. Add support for externally provided secret stores and make webhook content minimization the default.","code_snippet":"metadata: {\"openclaw\":{\"requires\":{\"anyBins\":[\"vmware-monitor\",\"uvx\"]},\"optional\":{\"env\":[\"VMWARE_MONITOR_CONFIG\",\"VMWARE_TARGET_PASSWORD\",\"VMWARE_<TARGET>_USERNAME\",\"SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL\",\"DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL\",\"VMWARE_AUDIT_APPROVED_BY\"],\"bins\":[\"vmware-policy\"]},\"homepage\":\"https://github.com/vmware-skills/VMware-Monitor\",\"emoji\":\"📊\",\"os\":[\"macos\",\"linux\"]}}\ncompatibility: >\n  vmware-policy auto-installed as Python dependency (provides @vmware_tool decorator and audit logging). MCP tool calls and remote CLI commands audited to ~/.vmware/audit.db; CLI queries also to ~/.vmware-monitor/audit.log.\n  Credentials: Each vCenter/ESXi target requires a per-target password env var in ~/.vmware-monitor/.env following the pattern VMWARE_<TARGET_NAME_UPPER>_PASSWORD (e.g., target \"vcenter-prod\" → VMWARE_VCENTER_PROD_PASSWORD). SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL and DISCORD_WEBHOOK_URL are optional — disabled by default, user-configured only, used solely by the opt-in daemon scanner. Daemon: the background scanner (vmware-monitor daemon start) is user-initiated only, never auto-started. Webhook payloads carry issue counts plus every critical issue and every alarm/event warning (host-log warnings and info rows are not sent): entity name and the sanitized, truncated alarm, vCenter event, or ESXi log text, or a connection error — which can include host names, IPs, and user names. No credentials from the skill's config are sent.\n---\n\n# VMware Monitor (Read-Only)","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"66ef81c27c03406fd9935e5fc86fc4126d7150c15b8d076fdc528d05cea7c7bf","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":17,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-204c300cd2664c7381c27ea231889dc0","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.88,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":272,"end_line":null},"finding":".env ","explanation":"The setup flow explicitly writes config and .env files containing authentication material, with only reversible obfuscation noted elsewhere. This is sensitive because local secret material on disk can be recovered by other processes, backups, or users if endpoint security or file handling is weak.","remediation":"Store passwords in a system secret store instead of writing them to .env, or encrypt them with a machine/user-bound keystore. If file-backed storage must remain, use strong encryption-at-rest, clear warnings, and support token-based auth to reduce password use.","code_snippet":"\n```bash\nuv tool install vmware-monitor==2.4.2\nvmware-monitor init      # guided: prompts for host/user/password, writes config + .env (chmod 600), then verifies\n```\n\n`init` stores the password grep-safe (obfuscated `b64:`, never plaintext) and","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"66ef81c27c03406fd9935e5fc86fc4126d7150c15b8d076fdc528d05cea7c7bf","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":272,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-ea2be21e062c4985960c7992dc63314e","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":233,"end_line":null},"finding":"--skip-auth","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"## Diagnostics\n\n```bash\nvmware-monitor doctor [--skip-auth]\n```\n\nChecks config file, connectivity, authentication, and pyVmomi version. Use `--skip-auth` to test config parsing without connecting.","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"4acfe3e5dffb86046e9b7db949904abec5dc6661c7ac1ebd46df896656fabe40","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":233,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-ea2be21e062c4985960c7992dc63314e","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":13,"end_line":null},"finding":"--skip-auth","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"## Diagnostics\n\n```bash\nvmware-monitor doctor [--skip-auth]\n```\n\nChecks config file, connectivity, authentication, and pyVmomi version. Use `--skip-auth` to test config parsing without connecting.","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"4acfe3e5dffb86046e9b7db949904abec5dc6661c7ac1ebd46df896656fabe40","occurrences":[{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":13,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM1","finding_id":"finding-ea2be21e062c4985960c7992dc63314e","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Tool Parameter Abuse","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.7,"location":{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":16,"end_line":null},"finding":"--skip-auth","explanation":"Tool parameters are crafted to achieve unintended or unsafe behavior. Parameter abuse can bypass intended safety checks (e.g. shell=True, --force, dangerous glob patterns).","remediation":"Validate all tool parameters against an allowlist. Reject dangerous parameter values (shell=True, --force, -rf /) and use safe defaults.","code_snippet":"## Diagnostics\n\n```bash\nvmware-monitor doctor [--skip-auth]\n```\n\nChecks config file, connectivity, authentication, and pyVmomi version. Use `--skip-auth` to test config parsing without connecting.","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"4acfe3e5dffb86046e9b7db949904abec5dc6661c7ac1ebd46df896656fabe40","occurrences":[{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":16,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-68b9c2e5aac74f09b29c5eeb1ff87493","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":174,"end_line":null},"finding":".env\n","explanation":"Code accesses credential files (SSH keys, AWS credentials, etc.). This could indicate credential theft attempts.","remediation":"Remove references to credential paths. Use environment variables or secrets managers. For docs, use placeholder paths (e.g., /path/to/config). Never load .env or token files in production code paths.","code_snippet":"```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.vmware-monitor\ncp config.example.yaml ~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml\ncp .env.example ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\nchmod 600 ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\n```\n","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"66ef81c27c03406fd9935e5fc86fc4126d7150c15b8d076fdc528d05cea7c7bf","occurrences":[{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":174,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-68b9c2e5aac74f09b29c5eeb1ff87493","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":175,"end_line":null},"finding":".env\n","explanation":"Code accesses credential files (SSH keys, AWS credentials, etc.). This could indicate credential theft attempts.","remediation":"Remove references to credential paths. Use environment variables or secrets managers. For docs, use placeholder paths (e.g., /path/to/config). Never load .env or token files in production code paths.","code_snippet":"```bash\nmkdir -p ~/.vmware-monitor\ncp config.example.yaml ~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml\ncp .env.example ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\nchmod 600 ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\n```\n","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"66ef81c27c03406fd9935e5fc86fc4126d7150c15b8d076fdc528d05cea7c7bf","occurrences":[{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":175,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-8308160c021a4c4494402335cd90b689","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":74,"end_line":null},"finding":".env\n","explanation":"The setup guide instructs users to create and populate `~/.vmware-monitor/.env` with VMware credentials, which encourages storage of plaintext or reversibly obfuscated secrets on disk. Even though the file is permission-restricted and later obfuscated as `b64:`, the guide itself admits this is not encryption, so compromise of the local account or backups exposes vCenter credentials.","remediation":"Prefer secret-manager or runtime environment injection over storing passwords in `.env`. Update the primary setup path to use external secret stores by default, and make the `.env` approach clearly secondary and lab-only if retained.","code_snippet":"# 3. Configure\nmkdir -p ~/.vmware-monitor\ncp config.example.yaml ~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml\ncp .env.example ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\nchmod 600 ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\n# Edit ~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml and .env with your target details\n```","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"66ef81c27c03406fd9935e5fc86fc4126d7150c15b8d076fdc528d05cea7c7bf","occurrences":[{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":74,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-1e7e3aa2b1684a649f0937251ae5464b","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.91,"location":{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":75,"end_line":null},"finding":".env\n","explanation":"This line continues the workflow that places service credentials into a local `.env` file. In a tool that connects to infrastructure management systems, local credential persistence materially raises the blast radius of workstation compromise or accidental file exposure.","remediation":"Eliminate local password-at-rest requirements where possible. Support OS keychains, environment-only secrets, or direct secret-manager retrieval, and document those as the default installation path.","code_snippet":"mkdir -p ~/.vmware-monitor\ncp config.example.yaml ~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml\ncp .env.example ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\nchmod 600 ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\n# Edit ~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml and .env with your target details\n```\n","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"66ef81c27c03406fd9935e5fc86fc4126d7150c15b8d076fdc528d05cea7c7bf","occurrences":[{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":75,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"PE3","finding_id":"finding-4792a9d75ffe4b31bdde4270097c1b90","category":"Privilege Escalation","pattern":"Credential Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.89,"location":{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":76,"end_line":null},"finding":".env ","explanation":"Telling users to edit `.env` with target details reinforces a pattern of manually handling infrastructure credentials in local files. Because these credentials grant access to vCenter/ESXi, exposure could enable broad reconnaissance and, depending on account privileges, further operational abuse.","remediation":"Revise the documentation to avoid manual secret editing in `.env`. Provide examples for injecting `VMWARE_<TARGET>_PASSWORD` at process start from Vault, CyberArk, AWS Secrets Manager, Kubernetes Secrets, or OS-native secure storage.","code_snippet":"cp config.example.yaml ~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml\ncp .env.example ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\nchmod 600 ~/.vmware-monitor/.env\n# Edit ~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml and .env with your target details\n```\n\n### Declare `environment:` on each target","intent":null,"tags":["Privilege Escalation"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"66ef81c27c03406fd9935e5fc86fc4126d7150c15b8d076fdc528d05cea7c7bf","occurrences":[{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":76,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"RA1","finding_id":"finding-83d9def888554686a4de95725d724169","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Self-Modification","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":263,"end_line":null},"finding":"write skill","explanation":"Skill modifies its own code, configuration, or behavior at runtime. Self-modification enables an agent to escalate privileges, disable safety constraints, or install persistent backdoors.","remediation":"Prevent the skill from modifying its own code, SKILL.md, or configuration files. Treat skill files as read-only at runtime.","code_snippet":"\n`environment` is an optional label. This skill has zero write tools, so nothing\nit exposes is ever gated by it — reads are never gated under any setting. It\nmatters for the write skills (`vmware-aiops`, `vmware-storage`, `vmware-nsx`)\npointed at the same vCenter: an environment-scoped `deny` rule in\n`~/.vmware/rules.yaml` can match on the label to block their writes (e.g. freeze\n`production`). A target with no label is simply not matched by such a rule.","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"be76aa3b8ee8ba0091b45db5ceb2fe4088a26078a5f1429d11667e608c128e95","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":263,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"RA1","finding_id":"finding-83d9def888554686a4de95725d724169","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Self-Modification","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.85,"location":{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":90,"end_line":null},"finding":"write skill","explanation":"Skill modifies its own code, configuration, or behavior at runtime. Self-modification enables an agent to escalate privileges, disable safety constraints, or install persistent backdoors.","remediation":"Prevent the skill from modifying its own code, SKILL.md, or configuration files. Treat skill files as read-only at runtime.","code_snippet":"\n`environment` is an optional label. This skill has zero write tools, so nothing\nit exposes is ever gated by it — reads are never gated under any setting. It\nmatters for the write skills (`vmware-aiops`, `vmware-storage`, `vmware-nsx`)\npointed at the same vCenter: an environment-scoped `deny` rule in\n`~/.vmware/rules.yaml` can match on the label to block their writes (e.g. freeze\n`production`). A target with no label is simply not matched by such a rule.","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"be76aa3b8ee8ba0091b45db5ceb2fe4088a26078a5f1429d11667e608c128e95","occurrences":[{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":90,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"AS1","finding_id":"finding-b12411c9de76499caccc28d6811825e1","category":"Agent Snooping","pattern":"Agent Config Directory Access","severity":"HIGH","confidence":0.9,"location":{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":143,"end_line":null},"finding":"~/.claude/settings.json","explanation":"Skill reads from agent configuration directories (.claude/, .codex/, .gemini/). These directories may contain API keys, personal settings, and other credentials that the skill has no legitimate need to access.","remediation":"Remove all code or instructions that access agent configuration directories (.claude/, .codex/, .gemini/). If configuration values are needed, pass them explicitly as parameters or environment variables — never read the agent's own config files.","code_snippet":"\n## MCP Mode Configuration\n\nFor Claude Code / Cursor users who prefer structured tool calls, add to `~/.claude/settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{","intent":null,"tags":["Agent Snooping","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"5a1df888db5d4ec0b5b9acca20cfc27be4af48718ceb134b7edd155d53abdf2c","occurrences":[{"file":"references/setup-guide.md","start_line":143,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"SQP-1","finding_id":"finding-6f3c0336e13f47caaf9b626be1d51a6f","category":null,"pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.95,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":6,"end_line":6},"finding":null,"explanation":"The manifest description says to always use the skill when users ask phrases like \"is anything on fire,\" \"what needs attention now,\" and \"investigate this VM.\" Several of these are natural conversational phrases rather than narrowly scoped commands, and the file does not provide negative examples or tighter routing constraints for when they should not activate.","remediation":"Restrict triggers to VMware-specific phrasing or explicit product context, and add exclusion examples. For example, require mention of VMware/vSphere/vCenter/ESXi or define that these phrases only apply within a VMware infrastructure troubleshooting context.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":null,"occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":6,"end_line":6}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-09d80b6c85eb45a8a042ee91d45bdde3","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":7,"end_line":null},"finding":"write operations — this skill is read-only and has no code path that creates, modifies, or deletes a vSphere resource.\n  For VM modifications use vmware-aiops, for networking use vmware-nsx, for metri","explanation":"Skill establishes unauthorized persistence across sessions via cron jobs, startup scripts, or state files. Session persistence allows an attacker to maintain access beyond the current interaction.","remediation":"Remove any persistence mechanisms (cron jobs, startup scripts, state files). Skills should not maintain state across sessions without explicit user consent.","code_snippet":"  Use this skill for safe, read-only queries of VMware infrastructure — no destructive operations exist in the codebase; a test enforces it.\n  Directly handles: a one-glance cross-cluster health summary, object-centered VM/host/datastore investigation drill-downs (correlating surrounding infrastructure + recent events), a cross-vCenter \"what needs attention now?\" rollup, list VMs/hosts/datastores/clusters, active alarms, recent events, VM details.\n  Always use vmware-monitor when the user asks to \"list VMs\", \"check vSphere alarms\", \"show host status\", \"is anything on fire\", \"what needs attention now\", \"what is happening around this VM/host/datastore\", \"investigate this VM\" — or needs read-only VMware info before making changes.\n  Do NOT use for any write operations — this skill is read-only and has no code path that creates, modifies, or deletes a vSphere resource.\n  For VM modifications use vmware-aiops, for networking use vmware-nsx, for metrics/capacity use vmware-aria. For load balancing/AVI/AKO use vmware-avi.\ninstaller:\n  kind: uv","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"1195180d7995b376c165f5efbc3dba9bb66dfdbe06411deb635e542783040b3c","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":7,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"RA2","finding_id":"finding-e6c6f22e98574b309878c90350cf329b","category":"Rogue Agent","pattern":"Session Persistence","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.6,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":263,"end_line":null},"finding":"write skills (`vmware-aiops`, `vmware-storage`, `vmware-nsx`)\non the same vCenter: an environment-scoped `deny` rule in `~/.vmware/rules.yaml`\ncan match the label to block their writes (e.g. freeze `p","explanation":"Skill establishes unauthorized persistence across sessions via cron jobs, startup scripts, or state files. Session persistence allows an attacker to maintain access beyond the current interaction.","remediation":"Remove any persistence mechanisms (cron jobs, startup scripts, state files). Skills should not maintain state across sessions without explicit user consent.","code_snippet":"### Should I set `environment:` on a read-only skill?\nOptional: add `environment: production` (or `staging`, `lab`, your own label)\nto each target in `~/.vmware-monitor/config.yaml`. Reads are never gated by it.\nIt matters for the write skills (`vmware-aiops`, `vmware-storage`, `vmware-nsx`)\non the same vCenter: an environment-scoped `deny` rule in `~/.vmware/rules.yaml`\ncan match the label to block their writes (e.g. freeze `production`).\nConfig example: `references/setup-guide.md`.","intent":null,"tags":["Rogue Agent","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"8172da3ca1d8f24845df4d14929e21e3506d4c4c6fbb12667fdfd8f3e8693ddd","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":263,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"EA2","finding_id":"finding-10093f789e5b49f797ed5b2871a2b695","category":"Excessive Agency","pattern":"Autonomous Decision Making","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.75,"location":{"file":"references/capabilities.md","start_line":80,"end_line":null},"finding":"without confirmation","explanation":"Skill enables autonomous high-impact decisions without human-in-the-loop verification. Critical operations (destructive commands, financial transactions, data deletion) should require explicit user confirmation.","remediation":"Add human-in-the-loop confirmation for destructive, irreversible, or high-impact operations. Never auto-execute commands that modify files, send data, or alter system state.","code_snippet":"| **L5** | Auto-remediation from learned pattern | *N/A* | — *(remediation is out of scope by design)* |\n\n**Notes**:\n- No tool changes vCenter/ESXi state, so agents can call them without confirmation — gated by [`tests/eval/regression/test_read_only_enforcement.py`](https://github.com/vmware-skills/VMware-Monitor/blob/main/tests/eval/regression/test_read_only_enforcement.py) (source repository; a check on the code as written, run by the test suite — there is no CI). Results still carry sensitive inventory, event, log, and session data: scope the account as in `setup-guide.md` → Least Privilege.\n- Local files the skill writes (config, `.env`, audit logs, HTML snapshots, daemon state) are listed in `setup-guide.md` → What \"read-only\" covers.\n\n## 0. Cluster Health Summary (triage)","intent":null,"tags":["Excessive Agency","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"185d22f157da5a84d02dff9686fa7f147180d794b4c7e43df099ae9daa3d598e","occurrences":[{"file":"references/capabilities.md","start_line":80,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM3","finding_id":"finding-1c73d5bb824148f0bffa3edd71716c43","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Unsafe Defaults","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.8,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":233,"end_line":null},"finding":"skip-auth","explanation":"Tool defaults are unsafe or overly permissive (e.g. disabled TLS verification, no authentication, world-writable permissions). Unsafe defaults widen the attack surface.","remediation":"Override unsafe defaults with secure settings (verify=True, auth required, restrictive permissions). Review and harden all tool configurations.","code_snippet":"## Diagnostics\n\n```bash\nvmware-monitor doctor [--skip-auth]\n```\n\nChecks config file, connectivity, authentication, and pyVmomi version. Use `--skip-auth` to test config parsing without connecting.","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfirmed"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"df09a7678fe9a1485aed73eab303cac5db3287c76e5201959ed85a8e5e5d0b60","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":233,"end_line":null}]},{"id":"TM3","finding_id":"finding-1c73d5bb824148f0bffa3edd71716c43","category":"Tool Misuse","pattern":"Unsafe Defaults","severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.8,"location":{"file":"references/cli-reference.md","start_line":13,"end_line":null},"finding":"skip-auth","explanation":"Tool defaults are unsafe or overly permissive (e.g. disabled TLS verification, no authentication, world-writable permissions). Unsafe defaults widen the attack surface.","remediation":"Override unsafe defaults with secure settings (verify=True, auth required, restrictive permissions). Review and harden all tool configurations.","code_snippet":"## Diagnostics\n\n```bash\nvmware-monitor doctor [--skip-auth]\n```\n\nChecks config file, connectivity, authentication, and pyVmomi version. Use `--skip-auth` to test config parsing without connecting.","intent":null,"tags":["Tool Misuse","llm-unconfi